CORE
main.c
--unwind 4 --rounds 3 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --pointer-check --alloc-check --memory-leak-check
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
main links one node behind `slot` and spawns a thread that links a second
one in front of it, so at the end `slot -> thread node -> main node` and
nothing is lost. The leak check at the end of main compares the last
allocation with `slot`, `slot->next`, `slot->next->next`, ... Main is
symexed before the thread, and in `slot->next->next` the inner pointer
`slot->next` was dereferenced against main's own points-to set, which
cannot hold the thread's node: its shared read, which tops the set up from
what other threads publish, came only after the dereference. So main's
node, now behind the thread's, was unreachable and reported leaked. The
shared pointers inside a dereferenced pointer are now topped up first.

Reported FAILED before the fix, SUCCESSFUL after. Reduced from
goblint-regression/09-regions_13-arraycollapse_nr (valid-memtrack).

See `nested_deref_published_node_leak_real`: the thread's node really is
lost, which must stay FAILED.
